.TH "HTMLLABELELEMENT.HTMLFOR" "3JS" "August 27, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
HTMLLabelElement.htmlFor \- HTMLLabelElement: htmlFor property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]htmlFor\f[B]\f[R] property of the
\f[CR]HTMLLabelElement\f[R] interface is the ID of the associated
label\(cqs control element.
It reflects the value of, and is used to set and get the \f[CR]for\f[R]
content attribute.
.SH VALUE
A string that contains the ID of the element associated with the
control.
.SH DESCRIPTION
An \f[CR]HTMLLabelElement\f[R] should be associated with a control
element so that clicking on the label activates the control.
The \f[CR]for\f[R] attribute, reflected in the \f[CR]htmlFor\f[R]
property, should contain the \f[CR]id\f[R] of the associated control
element.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] If this property has a value, the
\f[CR]HTMLLabelElement.control\f[R] property must refer to the same
control.
.RE
.PP
The \f[CR]htmlFor\f[R] attribute provides JavaScript access to the
\f[CR]for\f[R] attribute value.
\f[CR]htmlFor\f[R] is used instead of \f[CR]for\f[R] to avoid clashing
with the JavaScript \f[CR]for\f[R] reserved word (\c
.UR https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9379
this is no longer strictly necessary, so may change in the future
.UE \c
).
.SH EXAMPLE
.SS Basic usage
In this example, the \f[CR]<label>\f[R] element\(cqs \f[CR]for\f[R]
attribute value is \f[CR]username\f[R], which is linked to the
\f[CR]<input>\f[R] via its \f[CR]id\f[R] attribute.
We use the \f[CR]htmlFor\f[R] DOM property to access the \f[CR]for\f[R]
attribute value.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]label\f[R] for=\(dqusername\(dq>Enter your username:</\f[B]label\f[R]>
<\f[B]input\f[R] id=\(dqusername\(dq name=\(dqusername\(dq type=\(dqtext\(dq />
.EE
.IP
.EX
document.querySelector(\(dqlabel\(dq).htmlFor;
\f[I]// username\f[R]
.EE
